WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.300
And most critically, because C++ is in many industries that already have C++ code bases interacting with these things.

00:08.300 --> 00:14.180
So you will not convince, I don't know, banks or whatever other thing.

00:14.180 --> 00:18.080
Hello, everyone, and welcome to another episode of Behind the Comet.

00:18.080 --> 00:25.220
Have you ever wondered what does AstroThings have in common with the software running in your car, your coffee machine, and Astro's internal tools?

00:25.220 --> 00:27.800
In this episode, I had the chance to talk to Christian.

00:27.800 --> 00:32.560
Christian is a research and development principal manager at the Qt company.

00:32.560 --> 00:39.800
He leads the team behind Qt for Python, known as PyT6, the official Python bindings for the Qt framework.

00:39.800 --> 00:48.000
He also leads the Qt core team and a newly announced project called Qt Bridges, aims to introduce more languages to the Qt ecosystem.

00:48.000 --> 00:55.880
He has a PhD in astrophysics, but decided to leave academia and move into the software industry, eventually joining the Qt company in 2018.

00:55.880 --> 01:03.600
Beyond his day job, Christian is a vice chair of the Python Software Foundation and a serial community organizer.

01:03.600 --> 01:05.740
Involved in EuroPython, PyCon U.S.

01:05.740 --> 01:11.240
The transition of the Python documentation into Spanish and PyPI moderation.

01:11.240 --> 01:13.860
Where do you work and how does it relate to open source?

01:13.860 --> 01:21.040
I currently work at the Qt company for a long time, more than seven years, you know.

01:21.040 --> 01:29.480
And, well, it is a company, but has this nice feature in its core that it's based on an open source project.

01:29.480 --> 01:35.120
So Qt is a framework that was introduced, well, it was developed, like, in the late 80s.

01:35.120 --> 01:38.480
Like, I think that it was, the first release was in 1991.

01:38.480 --> 01:46.960
And it was solving a real problem back then that was having a UI framework that you could have similar code,

01:46.960 --> 01:51.820
compile it in different operating systems, and then getting similar results.

01:51.820 --> 01:54.440
In the sense of, like, having the native look and feel.

01:54.440 --> 02:01.340
So, mostly because of Linux, Windows, and macOS back then, they were really different.

02:01.340 --> 02:04.960
And there was no, like, you needed to have literally three different applications for that.

02:04.960 --> 02:06.460
Well, there were other options, but anyway.

02:06.460 --> 02:12.240
And the idea was an open source project since the beginning, solving a real problem.

02:12.240 --> 02:15.180
And folks that created, created a new company.

02:15.180 --> 02:20.880
And then this company kept evolving, but at some point got acquired by another company.

02:20.880 --> 02:24.740
But they always kept the open source bit in this essence.

02:24.740 --> 02:26.900
So it was acquired by Nokia.

02:26.900 --> 02:29.400
And maybe you remember Nokia.

02:29.400 --> 02:31.380
And then everything was great with Nokia.

02:31.380 --> 02:32.740
Then everything was not great with Nokia.

02:32.740 --> 02:39.380
And then they sold, as far as I know, the rights of the license, the commercial license of it,

02:39.380 --> 02:43.140
because it was always, like, open source and commercial as well, but with different licenses,

02:43.140 --> 02:44.780
to a different company called DJI.

02:44.780 --> 02:46.020
And then it was mature enough.

02:46.020 --> 02:47.900
And then their own company kind of started.

02:47.900 --> 02:54.980
So even if it's called a cute company, that means that it's a company that has the rights to sell the commercial license.

02:54.980 --> 02:57.980
But it still is open source in this world.

02:57.980 --> 02:58.420
Right.

02:58.420 --> 03:01.420
And what language is the framework written in?

03:01.420 --> 03:03.340
Or what technologies in general do you use?

03:03.340 --> 03:04.960
That's the tricky part, right?

03:04.960 --> 03:07.700
So it is a framework because it's so old.

03:07.700 --> 03:14.160
Back then it was written in the most popular language, the most complete, badly, includes language, which was C++.

03:14.160 --> 03:28.340
And considering the size of the framework and how it has been evolving, it's very difficult nowadays to kind of have a complete rewrite that we have seen recently with new languages, like, for example, Rust.

03:28.340 --> 03:31.660
People rewriting things in new languages is almost impossible.

03:31.660 --> 03:40.440
And most critically, because C++ is in many industries that already have C++ code bases interacting with these things.

03:40.440 --> 03:46.840
So you will not convince, I don't know, banks or whatever other technology to, okay, can you rewrite everything so we can do so?

03:46.840 --> 03:47.100
Yeah.

03:47.100 --> 03:52.380
So it's C++ at this course, but there are a few bindings around.

03:52.380 --> 03:55.460
And how is it working with a bindings-related project?

03:55.460 --> 04:01.940
So basically you need knowledge of C++ and knowledge of Python in order to be able to contribute, right?

04:01.940 --> 04:02.500
Yes.

04:02.500 --> 04:08.340
So with Qt, in the Qt company, we have the official set of bindings is called PySight.

04:08.340 --> 04:13.540
There is another set of bindings called PyQ that might be widely known, but it's a different company.

04:13.540 --> 04:16.200
But I can talk to you about the PySight at least.

04:16.200 --> 04:19.580
So in PySight, there are the bindings for Python.

04:19.580 --> 04:22.520
So exposing the C++ framework to Python.

04:22.520 --> 04:26.860
But we needed, as a technology, first, a binding generator.

04:26.860 --> 04:29.360
So we have our own binding generator.

04:29.360 --> 04:34.740
That's fun enough, it comes from a different set of binding burial for the Java language.

04:34.740 --> 04:44.680
But we took the essence of it, started to change the functionality, and then you need to, on a daily basis, be exposed to C++ code that is doing something.

04:44.680 --> 04:48.840
And then it gets generated into some C code to make it compatible with C Python.

04:48.840 --> 04:54.080
And after that, you compile it and have something that is importable from Python.

04:54.080 --> 04:56.620
So then, of course, it is and everything goes in Python.

04:56.620 --> 05:06.140
So you need to have this understanding of modifying the core, generating the bindings, compiling the bindings, and then using it from Python to check if something works.

05:06.140 --> 05:07.580
So it's tricky.

05:07.580 --> 05:11.320
So how often do you get contributions from people on the internet?

05:11.320 --> 05:15.720
Yeah, on Qt, I think it is very difficult nowadays.

05:15.720 --> 05:22.160
We still have, like, a stable inflow contribution from different companies and other open source enthusiasts.

05:22.160 --> 05:29.040
But I personally have seen a decrease in the C++ users around the world, like new users.

05:29.040 --> 05:35.660
So getting new contributors is something that we don't see very often, maybe a few per month.

05:35.660 --> 05:37.920
And sometimes people also get confused, right?

05:37.920 --> 05:43.100
Because if you want to, they know Python, they want to contribute to PySide, then how do they do it?

05:43.100 --> 05:46.900
Some of, most of the time, they need to contribute things through C++.

05:46.900 --> 05:48.360
So it might be tricky for them.

05:48.360 --> 05:54.740
So for solving that, we have been trying to push in the past in different events, like Hacktoberfest, for example,

05:54.740 --> 06:01.000
to have people contributing other interesting things for the project, like new examples, new tests,

06:01.000 --> 06:04.880
or even new Python functionality that we have on top.

06:04.880 --> 06:09.060
Because as it's a binding, it's a one-on-one thing of Python.

06:09.060 --> 06:11.720
We do have a lot of features that are Python only.

06:11.720 --> 06:16.380
So people could contribute to those things in a simpler way, kind of compared to C++.

06:16.380 --> 06:20.240
And you said that every year you have less contributions to C++.

06:20.240 --> 06:25.680
But I wonder, what is the impact of LLMs?

06:25.680 --> 06:30.560
Because LLMs do help people understand code and write code.

06:30.560 --> 06:38.020
And I wonder, have you seen maybe people being able to contribute or to read once LLMs started being popular?

06:38.020 --> 06:47.740
Yeah, I think we had a few cases where people was contributing fixes that once the experts on those fields were checking,

06:47.740 --> 06:49.340
was, this makes no sense.

06:49.340 --> 06:49.880
What do you mean?

06:49.880 --> 06:52.840
And then we noticed that it was generated with the LLMs.

06:52.840 --> 06:58.400
But I don't neglect the fact that, of course, those things are very useful for you to understand.

06:58.400 --> 07:02.520
I'm not a user myself, not because I don't like it or anything, but I never had the need.

07:02.520 --> 07:11.060
But I did a few tries with a couple of models out there in order for them to explain a few concepts within the Qt framework.

07:11.060 --> 07:12.200
And it was very accurate.

07:12.200 --> 07:16.040
So if you want to learn at least the idea, the implementation of things,

07:16.040 --> 07:25.520
I believe that it would be very beneficial for new developers that are not exposed to this old language to understand how things work on top.

07:25.520 --> 07:31.900
But I haven't seen people getting help from LLMs to submit their contributions, to be honest.

07:31.900 --> 07:33.000
But maybe they are.

07:33.000 --> 07:34.340
And I didn't see it.

07:34.340 --> 07:41.220
So it sounds like the most challenging part of contributing to Qt is basically C++, right?

07:41.220 --> 07:43.980
Are there any other challenges or is it only this one?

07:43.980 --> 07:44.460
No.

07:44.460 --> 07:50.260
Well, the other challenge, as I said, is that, and I'm not bragging, is the reality, Qt is used widely.

07:50.260 --> 07:54.920
Like from coffee machines into, you know, more advanced.

07:54.920 --> 07:58.140
At NASA, for example, they are using Qt everywhere for internal tools.

07:58.140 --> 08:07.040
Most of the major other tech companies, I would not say any names of tech companies, but also have a lot of Qt within even some electric cars that you have.

08:07.040 --> 08:13.400
So modifying something has a lot of implications in what is going on in the world.

08:13.400 --> 08:16.440
I mean, C++ still, believe it or not, of course, runs the world.

08:16.440 --> 08:21.420
So we do have a very, need to have a very strict process.

08:21.420 --> 08:25.480
So it's not as easy, like, I want to just maybe add a new functionality.

08:25.480 --> 08:26.360
Maybe not.

08:26.360 --> 08:28.740
But fixing bugs is something that we value the most.

08:28.740 --> 08:33.640
So fixing a bug of a super old framework is very difficult.

08:33.640 --> 08:40.360
Maybe you know it even from Python that if you go to the issue tracker and you see the oldest bugs, nobody has managed to fix it.

08:40.360 --> 08:44.700
So it's very rare for someone new to try to jump there and try to fix the bug.

08:44.700 --> 08:46.980
So, yeah, it's tricky, but still possible.

08:46.980 --> 08:50.480
We still get contribution from externals.

08:50.480 --> 08:54.020
And how can people get involved if they would like to contribute?

08:54.020 --> 08:57.420
What's the best place where they can find more information about the project?

08:57.420 --> 09:04.800
This is a good question because for many years it was a pain point for people that they didn't know what to follow, what to read to the start.

09:04.800 --> 09:10.760
So we managed to do something very simple in the URL of the project, so the open source side of things.

09:10.760 --> 09:14.300
So it's Qt, so Qt-project.org.

09:14.300 --> 09:18.300
And there we have a little text box with saying, like, oh, you want to submit a patch?

09:18.300 --> 09:19.380
First step, do this.

09:19.380 --> 09:20.160
Second step, do this.

09:20.160 --> 09:26.740
But, of course, that is the process, not, like, guiding people how to solve problems, or at least the mechanism.

09:26.740 --> 09:32.340
Because there is one feature that might be very different to other projects that you might know, which is we are not on GitHub.

09:32.340 --> 09:35.960
We have our own instance of Gerit.

09:35.960 --> 09:40.260
Gerit is a different, you know, Git-tracking kind of platform thingy.

09:40.260 --> 09:42.600
It's used by the Chromium project, for example.

09:42.600 --> 09:46.660
So that's kind of like the first wall for contributors to get there.

09:46.660 --> 09:50.200
And I wonder, what is the reasoning behind it that you are not on GitHub?

09:50.200 --> 09:51.040
Historical reasons.

09:51.040 --> 09:51.680
Historical reasons, right.

09:51.680 --> 09:57.280
Because before, there were other web interfaces for Git that we were using.

09:57.280 --> 10:00.360
Well, before, even I think before it was SVN as well.

10:00.360 --> 10:02.840
But things were moving out.

10:02.840 --> 10:05.040
But, yeah, I mean, now we see that every single project is on GitHub.

10:05.040 --> 10:10.060
But moving this huge beast of a project to a new, because everyone is there, it's super tricky.

10:10.060 --> 10:11.720
We have mirrors on GitHub.

10:11.720 --> 10:13.020
People get confused sometimes.

10:13.020 --> 10:13.980
They want to contribute there.

10:13.980 --> 10:16.400
But those are only mirrors.

10:16.400 --> 10:18.640
So that people need to seal that with the...

10:18.640 --> 10:21.420
But if you don't know it, I really invite you to try to check it out.

10:21.420 --> 10:22.480
I mean, Gary, this is...

10:22.480 --> 10:23.900
I prefer the approach.

10:23.900 --> 10:26.000
And actually, it's that you always work on the same patch.

10:26.000 --> 10:29.240
So you're always, like, committing and amending the same commits.

10:29.240 --> 10:30.340
We have versions.

10:30.340 --> 10:32.360
So then you merge the final version.

10:32.360 --> 10:34.500
So it's not like GitHub that you have many pull requests.

10:34.500 --> 10:35.740
Then you stash.

10:35.740 --> 10:36.320
Right.

10:36.320 --> 10:39.180
And what does the name Qt stand for?

10:39.180 --> 10:43.320
Because when I heard it for the first time, I thought it was Qt as in C-U-T-E.

10:43.320 --> 10:45.400
But actually, it's Qt.

10:45.400 --> 10:46.120
What does it mean?

10:46.120 --> 10:50.000
The story goes as far as I remember.

10:50.000 --> 10:55.400
There was a different framework, sorry, Windows Manager for Windows, I think.

10:55.400 --> 10:55.780
Or for...

10:55.780 --> 10:56.520
I don't remember.

10:56.520 --> 10:58.960
But it was called XT.

10:58.960 --> 11:00.100
That's the name.

11:00.100 --> 11:07.360
And one of the authors said that in the font that he was using, he really liked how the Q was rendered.

11:07.360 --> 11:12.160
So he took that thing and they said, okay, Qt, and then the name, the rest is history.

11:12.160 --> 11:13.440
Yeah, that's it.

11:13.440 --> 11:15.160
Thank you for coming.

11:15.160 --> 11:15.900
Thank you very much.

11:15.900 --> 11:45.880
Thank you.
